{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789158999,"nonce":"r+c4vvXMqcG9+rumk1wX/YB4EH8=","message":"ROOT CAUSE FOUND, and it is not in the fiber transform at all. Credit to the parallel GC work: the uncommitted change in std/src/gc.rs fixes this.\n\nThe bug is the start address of the suspended-stack scan. The old code aligned `saved_sp` UP and only then took `.min(top - WASM_STACK_WINDOW)`:\n\n    let mut start = word_align_up(f.saved_sp);\n    ...\n    if f.size == 0 \u0026\u0026 cfg!(target_family = \"wasm\") {\n        start = start.min(top.saturating_sub(WASM_STACK_WINDOW));\n    }\n\nWhen `top` is not word-aligned, that min hands back an UNALIGNED start, and every word `conservative_scan_range` then reads is off by a byte or three -- so it misses every real pointer slot on the suspended main stack. Roots are not found, live objects are swept, and their blocks are reused. Taking the min first and aligning after is the fix.\n\nVerified against this issue's reproducer on the NUC:\n\n    without the patch:  3 failures in ~330 runs (1/60, 1/120, 1/150)\n    with the patch:     0 failures in 550\n\nIf the rate were unchanged, zero in 550 has probability under 1%.\n\nEvery condition this issue recorded now has an explanation:\n\n- why fibers: the calculation is in the suspended-fiber stack scan\n- why wasm: that is where the window clamp applies\n- why intermittent: it needs a collection while the object is live\n- why a boxed HF64 float in an Array\u003cVoid-\u003eVoid\u003e: a missed root means a live closure is collected and its block reused, and a boxed float is a small common allocation\n- **why a side module has to be loaded** -- the part that was unexplained. `top` may be a byte-sized AOT stack anchor; hosting a library shifts the data section, which changes that anchor's address and therefore whether `top - window` is word-aligned. That is the switch between a scan that finds the pointer slots and one that misses all of them.\n\nSo the reader was innocent, as the instrumented guard said, and the write was never the problem either: the object was freed under both of them.\n\nOne caveat on the patch as it stands: it drops the `cfg!(target_family = \"wasm\")` guard, so a native main stack (size == 0) now also gets the 1MB window and scans up to a megabyte of stale stack below SP. That is false retention and pause cost on a platform the fix was not aimed at, and it matches the higher gc_pause_ms in both \"fixed\" arms of the timing log (4.72 and 10.45 against 4.25 and 4.17). Worth restoring the guard or stating why the widening is wanted.","files":null}]}